WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.040
It's time to tuck yourself away with&nbsp;
a perfectly splendid new story in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.600
the Haunting series featuring spooky&nbsp;
new ghosts and murderous matriarchs.
&nbsp;

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.360
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and&nbsp;
in this video we're diving beneath the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:17.440
surface of Bly Manor to explain everything&nbsp;
about the ending, the Lady of the Lake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:21.600
the haunted house and its ghosts,
plus what the final scene really means. 
&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:27.360
Obviously spoilers ahead, so take care.
Bly Manor's original matriarch, Viola, aka the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:31.600
Lady of the Lake, is at the heart of this story
and the eighth episode is crucial for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:34.640
understanding the origins&nbsp;
of this season's mysteries.
&nbsp;

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:37.920
Viola was a confident woman who,&nbsp;
contrary to the norms of the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.320
took charge of her own affairs and refused&nbsp;
to bow to either her husband or the Church.
&nbsp;

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.160
While her marriage was more of a business&nbsp;
arrangement, she loved her daughter dearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:49.120
clinging to her tightly as they&nbsp;
slept together each night.
&nbsp;

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:54.240
And true to her possessive nature, she warned her&nbsp;
child about those who might seek control over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.480
manor in the future:
"It is you.
&nbsp;

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:58.480
It is me.
It is us."
&nbsp;

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.560
That moment is the origin of the phrase&nbsp;
that gives ghosts like Peter Quint the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:06.800
permission they need to possess a living&nbsp;
person such as Rebecca Jessel or Miles.
&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.480
"It's you.
It's me.
&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:12.320
It's us."
We also see Viola's jealously and controlling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:17.120
traits in her relationship with her sister.
When she first notices a look between Perdita&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:20.640
and her husband, the beginnings of&nbsp;
a deadly lung disease manifest.
&nbsp;

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:25.360
Later on, she becomes violently jealous of&nbsp;
Perdita after she finds her dancing with Arthur,
&nbsp;

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:30.720
and it's no coincidence that Peter Quint acts in&nbsp;
a similar way in his relationship with Rebecca.
&nbsp;

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:35.760
The Lady of the Lake's stranglehold over Bly&nbsp;
Manor is only broken in the final episode when,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:40.000
desperate to save Flora from a watery&nbsp;
doom, Dani offers Viola permission to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.856
live in her body.
"It's you!
&nbsp;

00:01:41.856 --> 00:01:43.680
It's me!
It's us!"
&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:47.760
We don't know exactly why Viola agrees,&nbsp;
but it could be that Dani's body offered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.120
her the opportunity to escape&nbsp;
her endless cycle of purgatory,
&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:55.280
or perhaps what Dani said jolted some&nbsp;
faded memory of the words she'd said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:58.720
previously to her own daughter.
Either way, it seems that Dani's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:03.520
will is superior enough at that moment to keep&nbsp;
the Lady of the Lake repressed for a while.
&nbsp;

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:06.880
The moment also breaks Viola's&nbsp;
spell over all of the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:12.720
ghosts trapped at Bly, who now disappear.
And as Viola accepts Dani's invitation, the colour&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:18.800
of this eye here changes from blue to brown.
Before this moment, both Dani's eyes were blue;
&nbsp;

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.880
but from this point on, she&nbsp;
has one blue and one brown eye,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:28.560
a mark of the beast now living in her body.
Another detail that represents how Dani isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:33.200
entirely human any longer is the doll that&nbsp;
Flora gives to her when they say goodbye.
&nbsp;

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.680
"Are you sure you wanna give me this one?"
"You must have it.
&nbsp;

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:38.240
It's you."
Flora regarding Dani&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:44.000
as a doll in some way means deep down the little&nbsp;
girl senses something has changed in the au pair,
&nbsp;

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.880
because it contrasts sharply with what she said&nbsp;
to Dani when she first arrived at Bly Manor.
&nbsp;

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:50.880
"Is that me?"
"Why, no, silly.
&nbsp;

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.960
You're you.
It's just a dolly."
&nbsp;

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:57.920
Dani fears the spirit of Viola inside her&nbsp;
and explains to Jamie that it isn't peaceful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:00.400
but is filled with rage.
"She's waiting.
&nbsp;

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:03.360
And at some point...
she's gonna take me."
&nbsp;

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:07.360
The final episode's title "The Beast in&nbsp;
the Jungle" comes from the novella of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:11.600
the same name by Henry James in which&nbsp;
a man is seized by the fear that some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:16.720
unknown catastrophe lies in wait for him.
For Dani, she understandably fears the beast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:18.080
inside her;
however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:23.360
unlike the book's protagonist, Dani and Jamie vow&nbsp;
to make the most of whatever time they have left,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:27.040
travelling then setting up a life together.
"One day at a time is what we've got.
&nbsp;

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:29.360
It's what everybody's got&nbsp;
when you get down to it."
&nbsp;

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.600
And, similar to Viola, Dani discovers she&nbsp;
has a lot longer than she first thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:37.360
and she's able to live relatively&nbsp;
peacefully for over five years.
&nbsp;

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:42.640
By this time though, Dani feels that the beast&nbsp;
is drowning out her own feelings more and more,
&nbsp;

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:46.320
and one night when she wakes up to&nbsp;
discover she's about to choke Jamie,
&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.560
she understands the time has come when&nbsp;
it's too dangerous to stay another day.
&nbsp;

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:54.240
When Jamie discovers Dani has drowned&nbsp;
herself in the lake at Bly Manor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:58.960
she begs her to take her as well,
but unlike Viola's vengeful spirit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:03.600
Dani's soul refuses because, in contrast&nbsp;
to the previous Lady of the Lake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:09.600
Dani's actions are motivated by selfless love.
Jamie goes through life looking at reflections&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:14.880
in the hope that Dani might come back to her. 
When the older Jamie played by Carla Gugino, goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:20.080
back to her hotel room after the wedding, there's&nbsp;
a hint towards a happy-ish ending of sorts.
&nbsp;

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:25.440
She runs a bath and fills a sink with water,&nbsp;
leaves her room door ajar, then falls asleep.
&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:30.800
As the camera cuts to behind Jamie we see what&nbsp;
looks likes Dani's hand on Jamie's shoulder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:34.400
the clues being the ring on her&nbsp;
finger and the sweater sleeve.
&nbsp;

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:38.480
My interpretation of this shot is&nbsp;
that Dani isn't literally in the room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:43.120
but rather as Jamie fell asleep they were&nbsp;
both reunited in her dream or memory,
&nbsp;

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:46.400
while Dani's spirit or&nbsp;
ghost remains at Bly Manor.
&nbsp;

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:51.520
The clue to this is what the storyteller says to&nbsp;
the wedding party about Dani's ultimate fate.
&nbsp;

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:56.560
"She will merely walk the grounds of Bly,&nbsp;
harmless as a dove, for all of her days.
&nbsp;

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:01.040
Leaving the only trace of who she once was in&nbsp;
the memory of the woman who loved her most."
&nbsp;

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:04.720
In the show, we see that even living&nbsp;
characters like Flora can experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:09.520
an old memory in a dream world of sorts,
and I think that perhaps Jamie is tucked away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:14.080
in a memory with Dani in this final moment.
If you like this theory about the ending,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:17.920
then leave a thumbs-up and let me know&nbsp;
what you think in the comments below.
&nbsp;

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:22.640
Another cool detail about this final scene is&nbsp;
that it cleverly links back to the opening scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:27.760
of the very first episode where the storyteller&nbsp;
Jamie wakes up from her sleep at the hotel,
&nbsp;

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:31.360
which suggests that she has been repeating&nbsp;
this routine for several nights with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:35.040
the hope of reuniting with Dani.
And the way the end of the season in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:39.920
effect loops back to the beginning makes me&nbsp;
think of the idea from Hill House that time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:45.120
isn't always linear but can fold in on itself.
"I thought for so long that time was like a line.
&nbsp;

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:46.640
But I was wrong.
It's not like that at all.
&nbsp;

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:51.760
Our moments fall around us like rain."
Viola is also the origin of the strange time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:55.200
loops that many of Bly's ghosts&nbsp;
find themselves trapped in.
&nbsp;

00:05:55.200 --> 00:06:00.240
Despite her prognosis by a doctor that she would&nbsp;
only have months left to live, her will to cling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:04.080
to life is stronger and she remains alive a&nbsp;
full six years after she initially falls later.
&nbsp;

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:09.440
"I do not go."
Sick of her sister, Perdita decides to kill her;
&nbsp;

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:14.400
however, even then Viola's will keeps her&nbsp;
spirit trapped in the realm of Bly Manor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:18.640
locked away in the trunk of her clothes and&nbsp;
jewels that she had put away for her daughter.
&nbsp;

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:24.240
Here, in this self-made purgatory, Viola&nbsp;
lives the same day over and over, sleeping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:27.440
waking and waiting for someone&nbsp;
with the key to open the trunk.
&nbsp;

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:32.240
"And she admitted her room was a&nbsp;
dream, a construct, a lie preferred...
&nbsp;

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:35.840
to the truth of the trunk."
Although Viola's endless cycle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:41.360
is broken when Perdita opens the trunk, affording&nbsp;
Viola an opportunity for revenge on her sister,
&nbsp;

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:46.240
sadness consumes her when she feels abandoned&nbsp;
by her husband and daughter after they sell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:51.440
the house and toss the trunk into the lake.
But Viola's will still holds her to this world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:57.440
and she begins a new unending cycle of sleeping,&nbsp;
waking and this time walking from her watery new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:02.720
home to her daughter's bedroom and back again. 
Her power, even as a ghost, is the reason why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:06.720
everyone who dies on the manor grounds&nbsp;
is unable to leave and they end up as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:12.160
ghosts stuck in a limbo similar to hers.
"The pull of that next world ignored, she instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:17.120
made her own gravity, gravity of will, that&nbsp;
would change the terrain of Bly Manor forever."
&nbsp;

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:21.760
And it could be said that her power even&nbsp;
extends a little to the nearby town of Bly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:26.480
something alluded to in the first episode:
"The people here, most of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:30.240
they're born here, they die here.
The whole town is one big gravity well."
&nbsp;

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:34.400
Similar to Viola, each of the other ghosts&nbsp;
at Bly find themselves living in a loop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:38.960
of their own construction after they die.
They're able to hop around between memories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:43.280
but they always seem to end up returning&nbsp;
to some defining moment in their life.
&nbsp;

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:48.320
For Peter Quint, he always finds himself&nbsp;
returning to a moment which he describes as Hell,
&nbsp;

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:52.240
because he has to repeatedly see his&nbsp;
mother come to try and blackmail him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:56.320
the same person who enabled his father's&nbsp;
abuse of him and other children.
&nbsp;

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:01.040
"It's like I'm in hell."
"Well, where else would you go?"
&nbsp;

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:05.120
Miss Jessel finds herself returning to&nbsp;
a moment where Peter photographs her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:08.640
over and over while she's wearing&nbsp;
Charlotte Wingrave's fur coat.
&nbsp;

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:13.120
"Not this one, please."
"Let me show you how beautiful you are."
&nbsp;

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:16.720
Rebecca hates this memory, because&nbsp;
it's the time when she decided to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:21.440
along with Peter's lies against her better&nbsp;
judgement and despite Mrs Grose's warnings.
&nbsp;

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:25.760
This seminal moment ultimately led to&nbsp;
Peter completely deceiving her when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:30.080
takes possession of her body and drowns&nbsp;
her so that she ends up stuck with him.
&nbsp;

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:35.120
In Mrs Grose's case, she finds herself returning&nbsp;
to the moment when she first met Owen.
&nbsp;

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:39.920
Her loop is not as unpleasant as Peter's or&nbsp;
Rebecca's, perhaps because she doesn't suffer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:44.560
the same regret or guilt that Rebecca or Peter&nbsp;
do for their actions when they were alive.
&nbsp;

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:48.000
Her loop can be disconcerting for&nbsp;
her at times though, because she's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:52.400
not aware to begin with that she's dead,
but she's essentially spending time with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:55.440
the person who she grows to love&nbsp;
and who becomes her soulmate.
&nbsp;

00:08:56.080 --> 00:09:00.960
A theme that becomes clear by the end of Bly Manor&nbsp;
is that the story we've seen isn't just a ghost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:03.760
story, but also a love story.
"Same thing, really."
&nbsp;

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:09.120
As creator Mike Flanagan told Vanity Fair, the&nbsp;
show "provides a new way to tell a love story"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:14.400
and "by the end [of the season], it's really&nbsp;
hard to differentiate tragedy [from] romance.
&nbsp;

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:19.360
That sense of romantic longing for someone who&nbsp;
meant so much to us – but who's gone – really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:23.040
is the heart of any ghost story."
And as we see over the season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:27.840
Bly Manor includes many types of love,&nbsp;
from healthy relationships to toxic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:34.080
and guilt-ridden romances, each with their own&nbsp;
power to either exorcise or create new ghosts.
&nbsp;

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:38.880
When Dani and Jamie finally recognise their&nbsp;
true feelings for each other, this moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:44.400
exorcises the ghost of her dead fiancé Edmund&nbsp;
which has been haunting her since he died.
&nbsp;

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:48.400
The day after Jamie and Dani sleep&nbsp;
together, Dani is finally able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:52.320
look in a mirror without seeing his&nbsp;
ghost staring back accusingly at her.
&nbsp;

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:56.800
Dani's true love for Jamie helps her&nbsp;
let go of her guilt over his death.
&nbsp;

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:00.480
As Mike Flanagan has said, in the&nbsp;
Haunting anthology series ghosts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:04.960
are "an expression of emotional wounds"
and through her relationship with Jamie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:08.880
Dani is finally able to heal&nbsp;
her trauma from Edmund's death.
&nbsp;

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:13.520
Likewise, it's Dani's selfless love for&nbsp;
Flora and willingness to sacrifice her own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:18.000
life when she invites Viola to possess&nbsp;
her which exorcises all of the ghosts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:22.320
that have been trapped at Bly Manor.
In contrast to Dani and Jamie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:27.200
Peter Quint and Rebecca Jessel's relationship&nbsp;
is particularly toxic and filled with jealousy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:30.400
deceit, and manipulation.
One of the manifestations of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:34.800
this is how the pair take possession of Miles&nbsp;
and Flora often without their permission,
&nbsp;

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:39.040
something we see through the children's&nbsp;
strange behaviours such as Miles demanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:44.000
to be given wine, and smoking and talking&nbsp;
in a manner untypical for a boy of his age.
&nbsp;

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:55.760
Peter is the principal toxic&nbsp;
force in the relationship
&nbsp;

00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:59.040
and we see him abuse the trust&nbsp;
Rebecca places in him when she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:03.200
gives him permission to take control of her&nbsp;
body so that they can be together forever.
&nbsp;

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:08.080
Her stipulation was that they be equals,&nbsp;
however, without asking for her consent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:13.440
he selfishly drowns her in the lake, trapping&nbsp;
her as a ghost with him forever at Bly Manor.
&nbsp;

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:18.480
A little detail foreshadowing just how bad&nbsp;
Peter Quint will end up being for Miss Jessel
&nbsp;

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:23.040
is when the song "Tainted Love" plays&nbsp;
in episode 3 just before he and Henry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:28.000
interview Rebecca for the au pair job.
And bound up with this idea of toxic love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:32.880
is the theme of possession and ownership.
The two most destructive ghosts at Bly Manor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:37.520
Peter Quint and Viola, are obsessed with&nbsp;
trying to control the people around them.
&nbsp;

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:42.640
In fact, in many ways, Peter doesn't treat&nbsp;
Rebecca like an autonomous human being with wants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:46.480
needs, and rights of her own;
rather, he treats her like property;
&nbsp;

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:50.800
he sees what he wants and takes it without&nbsp;
permission, whether that's draining Henry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:54.800
Wingrave's bank accounts, stealing&nbsp;
jewellery and fur coats from Bly Manor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:59.200
or taking Rebecca's life forever.
And the way Peter manipulates people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:04.240
to do exactly what he wants is like a child&nbsp;
playing with a toy, or like Flora playing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:06.344
her dolls, as Mike Flanagan says.
As we know from Peter's hell loop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.344 --> 00:12:08.800
he was similarly dehumanised as a child.
However, as Dani explains, treating people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:12.640
like this is the antithesis of true love.
"People do, don't they?
&nbsp;

00:12:12.640 --> 00:12:15.760
Mix up love and possession.
I don't think that should be possible.
&nbsp;

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:18.800
I mean, they're opposites, really.
Love and ownership."
&nbsp;

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:23.520
When grown-up Flora tells the storyteller that&nbsp;
she's scared of losing her forever person,
&nbsp;

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:27.760
Jamie replies that they should enjoy their&nbsp;
time together and not worry about that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:32.320
and when that moment comes, she can still&nbsp;
enjoy the memories of their time together,
&nbsp;

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:35.920
and, as I said earlier, that's&nbsp;
something we see Jamie do herself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:39.600
at the very end of the season.
It's also something the older&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:43.440
Owen talked about in his speech at the&nbsp;
rehearsal dinner in the first episode.
&nbsp;

00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:47.680
"To truly love another person is to&nbsp;
accept that the work of loving them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:52.000
is worth the pain of losing them."
Mrs Grose proves this point at the end, when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:56.880
she selflessly decides to leave the memory she's&nbsp;
in with Owen to help him in the real world. 
&nbsp;

00:12:56.880 --> 00:13:01.200
"Be brave in death, Hannah.
We both know you're not so selfish...
&nbsp;

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:04.480
to remain hidden in a memory."
Likewise, Dani's Lady in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:09.840
Lake refuses to drag Jamie down with her. 
And, similarly, in the end, Rebecca manages to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:14.800
reject Peter's manipulations and helps protect&nbsp;
Flora by refusing to possess her as well.
&nbsp;

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:19.360
The other main love story at Bly&nbsp;
Manor is between Henry and Charlotte,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.360 --> 00:13:22.800
the wife of his brother Dominic.
After Dominic discovers what's been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:26.560
going on, he and Charlotte go on a trip&nbsp;
to India to repair their marriage where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:31.200
they're tragically killed in an accident.
The tremendous guilt that Henry suffered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:35.680
when he heard the news and the knowledge that&nbsp;
he was indirectly responsible for their deaths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:40.400
gave birth to his doppelganger ghost that night.
And this ties in with what Mike Flanagan has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:44.240
said that "when we fall in love,&nbsp;
we give birth to a new ghost."
&nbsp;

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:49.520
Henry's antagonistic alter ego ghost was the&nbsp;
product of the guilt and sadness he felt upon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:53.840
learning of the death of the woman he loved.
And similar to the ghosts at Bly Manor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.840 --> 00:13:58.080
Henry seems to live much of his life in&nbsp;
a loop, even though he's still alive.
&nbsp;

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:01.840
His brother might have forbidden him&nbsp;
from returning to Bly after his affair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:05.920
but Henry's overbearing sense of guilt&nbsp;
means he doesn't even leave his office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.920 --> 00:14:10.480
at night to go to his home in London.
He also feels compelled to repeatedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:15.200
call Bly Manor, making him the cause&nbsp;
of the silent calls at the house.
